WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:12.501
[Music]

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:39.280
[Registered Nurse:] Okay, next patient, Mr. Silva. 40 percent. Title volume&nbsp;800. Rate 10....five. Last gas, CO2 was up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.920
Why does the resident have all that stuff on? Wait&nbsp;a minute. There's blood and body fluid precautions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:58.400
Who's the attending? Dr. Daniels, Infectious&nbsp;Disease. Oh great!  This guy's got AIDS!

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:07.111
They never tell, Respiratory Care anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;Well it was bound to happen sooner or later.&nbsp;

00:01:07.441 --> 00:01:11.040
[Clinical Instructor:] We want to focus on one particular aspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.120
of the AIDS crisis so to speak. The emotional&nbsp;reactions that people have, the emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.160
overreactions that people have, and concerns with&nbsp;working with AIDS patients. 

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:30.283
[Nurse 1:] The doctors aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;always honest, about the patient having AIDS.  We've&nbsp;had them not tell us before.  &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:40.576
[Clinical Instructor:] The fear of dishonesty or not full disclosure. [Nurse 2:] What is the medical world&nbsp;not telling us? 

00:01:40.576 --> 00:01:50.800
[Clinical Instructor:] Is the medical community telling&nbsp;&nbsp;all? What else? [Nurse 3:] You have to deal with whatever&nbsp;comes through the door. You don't know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.200
background of these people. Anyone is a potential&nbsp;AIDS patient. As well as, you know, having any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.880
number of problems. And it's not always a very&nbsp;clean business. It gets pretty messy sometimes.&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.480
[Nurse 4:] You don't want to do mouth-to-mouth on anybody. &nbsp;[Clinical Instructor:] Yeah,  yeah.  [Nurse 4:] Because you don't know who's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.214
exposed and it's not telling. Or who's been exposed&nbsp;and they don't know it.  

00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:17.680
[Practitioner:] My husband called me one&nbsp;&nbsp;night at work. And I told him I was taking care of&nbsp;an AIDS patient. We have a newborn. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.713
He told me to leave my clothes at the back door, he'd burn him when he&nbsp;got home. 

00:02:21.713 --> 00:02:27.360
[Nurse 5:] We have some departments that will come&nbsp;onto the floor and they'll see a blood and body&nbsp;secretion isolation, precaution label on the door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.520
Everything they need to follow is right there.&nbsp; But they want to know what the patient has. 

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:37.120
[Clinical Instructor:] Oh&nbsp;of course, because they want to know so that then&nbsp;they can freak out. Exactly. Tell me more. What&nbsp;else?

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:43.200
[Nurse 6:] There's still those group of questions that&nbsp;are not answered. The disease isn't old enough.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.225
How long is it gonna stay latent? When can it activate?&nbsp; There are a lot of things out there that we just&nbsp;don't know.

00:02:49.404 --> 00:02:53.280
[Clinical Instructor:] I think there are two issues. And&nbsp;I say there's two issues, it's not either/or.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.000
One is to have the information, the&nbsp;education, the knowledge,

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.480
the wisdom&nbsp;&nbsp;to have the policies and procedures all clearly&nbsp;laid out to the best that you possibly can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.760
But the second issue is I can guarantee,  you can&nbsp;talk till you die, of the facts. And if somebody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.553
gonna be overly emotional and over-reactive? It's just like talking to a wall. 

00:03:19.553 --> 00:03:24.880
[Nurse 7:] Our first contact&nbsp;with the patient, we're just sort of cold. I mean we accept them and then all of a sudden

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:31.712
you start looking through the history and you&nbsp;find out. Oh, this patient is a possible AIDS. And your mind starts racing, oh my goodness what do I do.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.460
And suddenly you know you change your attitude. 

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:41.638
[Clinical Instructor:] What if I get it myself. That's the bottom line issue. We've got all these other dimensions,

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:48.323
all these&nbsp;other factors that we worry about, the policies&nbsp;and the procedures and the information and the nature of the patient and all that kind of&nbsp;stuff.

00:03:48.323 --> 00:03:51.760
But the only reason why we're really worried&nbsp;about all this kind of stuff, is the bottom line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.920
Can I possibly get it? Now, how do you keep it in&nbsp;its perspective. How do you do that mentally?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.080
How do you do that emotionally? See the worst&nbsp;thing in the world would be, to say, "oh come on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:09.040
there's no problem. Come on it's really safe. All&nbsp;these people are just trying to make waves." 

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:16.720
Well, the truth is, there is that tiny little window. That&nbsp;window opens up a possibility not to have a cold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:27.004
Not to have a sniffle. But it's a long and painful&nbsp;death, for most patients. When you raise the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.004 --> 00:04:34.684
stakes to that level of seriousness. That little&nbsp;window suddenly becomes an enormous issue.

00:04:34.684 --> 00:04:42.560
[Practitioner:] Dear sis,&nbsp;&nbsp;I'm about to leave for work. Dan and the kids are&nbsp;still in bed. I'm working ICU now and I like it a&nbsp;lot.

00:04:43.781 --> 00:04:48.720
But Dan, is really giving me a hard time about&nbsp;the AIDS patients. He's convinced that I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.040
to bring the virus home and expose the kids. I've&nbsp;tried to talk to him about the precautions we take.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.040
But he just keeps insisting that I go back to PEDS. I'm not sure how I'm going to deal with this. 

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.080
[Nurse 8:] I&nbsp;don't talk about it to my husband.  Which isn't fair because if I, if, you know, if something bad happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.360
at work and I go home and I can tell him. You know. I had a baby die today and he like, "oh I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:17.600
so sorry." You know, "are you feeling okay about it&nbsp;now?" But if I had to go work with an AIDS patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.080
that was very ill and had a lot of secretions&nbsp;and I was kind of uptight about it. I can't go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.480
home and tell him. [Clinical Instructor:] That one of your major support&nbsp;systems isn't available to you in this context.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.880
[Husband:] So what's the matter with you? [Wife/Nurse:] the one in 14. His&nbsp;boyfriend or lover or whatever you call it,  it's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.840
in the room with him for about 15 minutes.&nbsp;They're just hugging, oblivious to the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.280
[Husband:] Well they've been together for nine years, the guy doesn't have much longer to live.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.680
[Wife/Nurse:] I know that, but it doesn't change the way I feel!

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.680
[Clinical Instructor:] What about the business of homosexuality&nbsp;and people's personal reactions to it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.040
Some of you may say hey I don't have any&nbsp;problem with it at all. It doesn't bother me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.800
at all. Somebody wants to be gay, if somebody wants&nbsp;to be heterosexual, bisexual, I couldn't care less.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.640
Some people have said I just kind of don't like.&nbsp;It makes me uncomfortable. Some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.280
say, "it's downright sick. &nbsp;
They deserve what they got, the bastards!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:21.440
It comes out in that whole range. In many cases&nbsp;you'll hear people saying that, "This is the Lord's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:27.680
Wrath, being brought down, upon those people!"&nbsp;Do you ever find yourself starting to worry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.320
about that stuff? A particular patient that&nbsp;you know just passed through. Maybe had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.720
indication that they were high-risk patient&nbsp;for some reason. [Nurse 3:] Makes me extremely disgusted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:48.800
with that whole sect of the population. Then I worry that I can't provide the level of care&nbsp;for that population group. That would, you know, that&nbsp;you would be perfectly capable of providing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.480
anyone else. [Clinical Instructor:] Yeah, and that's tough to deal&nbsp;with, I mean you're a professional person too. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.840
you still have to deal with your own personal&nbsp;reactions. But what we're really concerned about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.200
here, is how do you make sure that you're&nbsp;not exacerbating the emotional overreaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:11.520
Nobody's denying that it's serious. Nobody's&nbsp;denying that there's that tiny little window.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.880
How do you deal with that window in a functional&nbsp;effective rational manner? That's the issue that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:23.120
the focus here. Now let me show you something&nbsp;about that. We've got, (point) A's here. Now (point) A's are specific

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:34.960
situations. That you run into, that you have to&nbsp;deal with there's specific situations that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.320
run into on a day-to-day basis. It might be having&nbsp;to draw blood. It might be having somebody come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.120
into the emergency room with several symptoms, that might be indicative of an AIDS patient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:58.720
Now (point) C's, represent two things, your feelings&nbsp;and your behavior. Now, in that situation, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:04.000
you respond to that situation. Now there's four&nbsp;screwball feelings. There's four screwball feelings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.480
that, if you allow yourself to experience one of&nbsp; these four feelings, you are going to not handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.520
the situation as effectively as you could. You&nbsp;will overreact. The first one is, 'excessive anxiety'.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:20.720
If you get yourself overly anxious, uptight, worried, upset, you could call it a lot of different words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.480
But if you get yourself, excessively anxious, you're&nbsp;going to overreact and not handle that situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:30.640
as effectively or as professionally as you'd&nbsp;like. The second one is, 'excessive anger'. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:36.000
get yourself overly angry, disgusted, outraged.&nbsp;You are not going to handle that situation or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:44.160
that patient as effectively or professionally as&nbsp;you'd like. The third one, I call it, 'depression'.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.600
But a lot of people just call it, burnout. There's&nbsp;no real strong emotional response. It's just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:58.640
I've had it. The fourth is, 'guilt'. It's&nbsp;
there. Let's say that the person at, point C,

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.240
gets angry. [Husband:] You didn't say&nbsp;
anything to either of them, did you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:12.640
[Wife/Nurse:] I didn't say a word, [Husband:] But what did you do? [Wife/Nurse:] I did&nbsp;the job I was supposed to do and then I left.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.360
[Clinical Instructor:] Now it might be subtle. It might be what&nbsp;psychologists call, 'passive aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:24.480
behavior'.  You know, just the abrupt "boom, boom,&nbsp;boom", not being quite as sensitive. Certainly not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.360
being responsive to the emotional state of the&nbsp;patient. See those are the behaviors at point C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.840
You could take any one of these things, dealing&nbsp;with your spouse or relatives. What's the feeling?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.800
[[Practitioner:] It sounds crazy, but at times I actually start to&nbsp;feel guilty about caring for our AIDS patients.&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.800
Dan's got this notion in his head that I can&nbsp;pick and choose who I care for. I can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.080
imagine what he'd say, if he saw me doing some&nbsp;of the procedures that I have to do. I know he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:00.560
absolutely blow up, to make matters worse. He's been&nbsp;talking to mom and she's starting to ask questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:06.080
That's all I need, to have the whole family on my&nbsp;case. [Clinical Instructor:] Let's say, that you're simply uh, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:12.960
time you had to deal with an AIDS patient. What was&nbsp;the likely feeling? You know, the first time? Yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:22.160
anxiety, right. [Practitioner:] Now calm down. I've got to do this by&nbsp;the numbers. Thank God, this guy's really out of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.560
Should Ii call my supervisor? Maybe&nbsp;
I should get someone to assist?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.400
No, that's ridiculous! Come on, calm down! You've&nbsp;done a thousand blood gases. [Clinical Instructor:] If I said to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.520
if you want to make sure, that you handle these&nbsp;situations in a professional manner. Very simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:48.320
Just make sure that you never get yourself, excessively anxious, angry depressed or guilty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.440
Piece of cake right? Done, no problem. It's&nbsp;not that simple, is it? This is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:01.920
people make the mistake. All the time, all&nbsp;the time most people say that (point) A's cause (point) C's.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.720
That these are the things that make me anxious&nbsp;or maybe upset or maybe angry. But believe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:16.000
or not, A's do not cause C's. We think that way.&nbsp;We talk that way. We explain our world that way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:22.480
We say, "gee, you seem kind of freaked out. You&nbsp;seem kind of agitated today. What's the matter?"&nbsp;

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:25.680
"Well I just found out I've been working with&nbsp;an AIDS patient and a doctor knew it all along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:32.000
and didn't tell me!" Say well, "gee you know, uh gee,&nbsp;you seem kind of upset. What's the matter, Carol?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.960
Well gee, "I mean, I just had this exchange&nbsp;with my husband on the phone and he told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.560
me to burn my clothes.  You know just&nbsp;
because I was working with an AIDS patient. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:44.320
though (point) A cause (point) C. A don't cause C.  If&nbsp;A's don't cause C's, what do cause C's?

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:56.400
B's! Brilliant, but what are (point) B we run into a&nbsp;situation, any of these or any of the day-to-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.520
examples of these things. What happens at point&nbsp;B, before you wind up feeling it acting, at point C?

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:12.720
You think, the way you think, will determine how you&nbsp;wind up handling the situation. The way you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.640
about it. I don't mean the facts. The facts&nbsp;would help you to think rationally about it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:24.320
I can guarantee I've seen lots of people who had&nbsp;the facts still don't think rationally. And there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.000
three screwball ways of thinking. Actually there's&nbsp;only four ways of thinking in the whole world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.280
That's the good news because it's nice&nbsp;
and easy to remember just four of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:36.640
The bad news is that, three out of the&nbsp;
four ways that you could think, stink.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:44.480
The first type of thinking is called, 'awfulizing'. We awfulize about all kinds of things. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:50.560
perfect context to look at awfulizing. Every&nbsp;awfulized thought could be started with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.560 --> 00:13:00.320
phrase, "what if, dot, dot, dot." Practitioner:] What if I don't get&nbsp;the artery on the first try? What if he... Calm down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:13.600
What if I get a needle stick? Now concentrate.&nbsp;There, there's the artery. There it is.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.840
[Clinical Instructor:] The second one,

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:30.560
'shooting'. Some of us are absolutely&nbsp;
dynamite at, shooting. We should on ourselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:38.160
We should on other people. How about some of the ways that we should on the homosexual? [Wife/Nurse:] Look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:43.520
I don't care what they do with the door closed.&nbsp;I just don't see why, we should have to watch!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.640
[Husband:] Joan, you really should try and develop a little&nbsp;more of a tolerant attitude. [Wife/Nurse:] Wait a minute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:54.160
Let's get something straight here. He shouldn't&nbsp;have been doing those things in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.800
We shouldn't have to put up with&nbsp;
the very thing, that caused this disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:10.240
[Clinical Instructor:] Third one, is 180 degrees opposite, 'rationalizations'.&nbsp;
Rationalizations, are where you do the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:15.440
avoidance kind of thing. Some of the things we&nbsp;were talking about before. "So what, who cares. Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:22.080
deal. Doesn't bother me." How do you rationalize, the&nbsp;conflicts in your relationship with your spouse?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:29.680
Versus your relationship to your profession? You have to rationalize it in order to, say well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:35.520
"we just don't talk about it." Practitioner:] I really feel like&nbsp;I'm caught in a bind. I'm determined to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:41.600
Critical Care Nursing. It's challenging and I'm&nbsp;good at it I don't know how I'll feel in a year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:47.520
We've lost seven AIDS patients on our&nbsp;
unit since I started and that's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:53.200
taken a toll on all of us. Course it's&nbsp;
impossible for me to talk to Dan about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.640 --> 00:15:00.160
for the time being. I'm not saying anything at home&nbsp;about AIDS. It's just not worth the aggravation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:00.720
 

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:05.680
[Clinical Instructor:] That gets you through today, doesn't it? That gets&nbsp;
you through the incident. The rationalizations, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:11.120
cons. But they sure don't get you through the&nbsp;ongoing process that you have to deal with in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:18.160
the long run. At some point you got to deal with&nbsp;the real questions and you have to be real clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:23.440
about what it is, that you're going to think. Not&nbsp;just what facts you know. Not just what procedures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:29.360
you know. But how am I going to deal with it&nbsp;emotionally? There's a fourth option, fortunately.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:40.640
It comes out, well, it's what's called, the 'rational&nbsp;option' and they come out like this. I want... I like...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:57.680
I'd prefer...  If.. Would be better if, dot dot dot. I'd&nbsp;like to have perfect knowledge. I'd like to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:03.760
procedures that are absolutely guaranteed, that&nbsp;if, I follow these procedures there's absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:11.840
no way, no way possible, that anything could happen&nbsp;to me. I'd like all AIDS patients to be charming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:17.040
scintillating and thoroughly enjoyable to work&nbsp;with and totally in agreement with my philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:24.000
of sexuality. If that was the case, "great!" If it's&nbsp;not, is it awful, terrible, horrible the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:29.280
world and I get to make myself excessively anxious,&nbsp;angry, depressed, guilty, frustrated, upset, hassled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:36.960
harried, irritated and wind up not giving the kind&nbsp;of care that I have committed myself to giving? No.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:44.320
It's unfortunate. I regret it. I'm concerned&nbsp;about it and I'm committed to doing a good job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:50.080
[Nurse 9:] I think it's great, I mean you know the theory&nbsp;itself is is fine. But in a highly stressful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:55.840
situation, like in a healthcare situation, um, it&nbsp;could be, I could see where it could be difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:01.360
to apply.  I mean even if you've got this down&nbsp;pat and you've thought it out. [Clinical Instructor:] Absolutely. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:07.040
absolutely right, The point that you said&nbsp;it very tactfully, as opposed to saying, "yeah but Ii&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:14.480
don't think it's going to work in this situation." I&nbsp;think the reality is, that it is harder to do it, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:20.880
the crisis situation. The stakes are larger, the&nbsp;moment is quicker and sudden. Consequently it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:26.000
even more dramatically important, that you learn&nbsp;how to get control of it, instead of it running you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:30.320
And by the way it's not just in a medical&nbsp;situation. Anytime you're in any kind of crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:38.080
situation, your first inclination is to overreact.&nbsp; Does overreacting prevent the acquisition of AIDS?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:43.120
Does it make you feel any better? [Nurse 3:] It&nbsp;may help relieve some of the anxiety.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:49.680
[Clinical Instructor:] Does it make it go away? [Nurse 3:] It doesn't make it go&nbsp;away. But it helps, um, helps you to feel better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:54.400
if you have a healthy outlet to ventilate your&nbsp;feelings. [Clinical Instructor:] Yes exactly and I really believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:58.240
that some people believe, "that if I can just get&nbsp;it off my chest", and maybe I can put it put it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:03.200
to rest and and then go on and do my business.&nbsp;My only concern is that isn't what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:06.800
with a lot of people. Is that they do that and then&nbsp;they go off and they don't do their business in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:12.640
professional manner, because of all the stuff that&nbsp;they did agitating themselves. [Nurse 10:] We may work through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:19.040
this rational thoughts to ourselves and everything&nbsp;be just fine. Yet, when you're actually ready to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:24.800
a procedure or do something with the patient.&nbsp;To have a physician or a nurse who you respect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:30.880
at the last minute say, "I wouldn't do that." It just&nbsp;takes so little to have that irrational thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:40.880
creep back up and build. [Clinical Instructor:] Physicians probably&nbsp;
have just as many wacko overreactions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:46.800
AIDS patients as any other population. The&nbsp;physicians ought to be talking about, "What the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:53.520
heck is my feeling," about this person. What are my&nbsp;biases? What are my fears. You know because in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:58.800
they probably set a standard for everybody&nbsp;else. They can throw a curveball in there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:03.040
If you really got it together and somebody&nbsp;throws one of those curveballs in there, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:11.200
you're talking about and can set the whole unit,&nbsp;reeling in uncertainty. Okay now I want to go over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:17.120
the three steps for 'Changing Your Thinking', if&nbsp;you find yourself overreacting or that you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:23.520
be overreacting to a particular situation. Let's&nbsp;say that you find yourself getting agitated, uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:27.840
overactive in some situation. You're having to&nbsp;deal with something that you feel strongly about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:31.760
So the first question is, what are you thinking&nbsp;to yourself, about yourself? What are you thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:35.520
to yourself about the others in the situation&nbsp;and what are you thinking about the situation?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:43.600
The second step in the process is to ask yourself&nbsp;two questions. What's true? What is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:48.640
verifiably true? What are the facts?  Then you&nbsp;ask yourself the second question, what's not true?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:53.680
The third step is very simple, you teach&nbsp;
yourself to think in terms of preferences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.400 --> 00:20:01.120
Coming down to step three, when you substitute&nbsp;the the realistic preferences. You're not getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:07.280
rid of your feelings. What you're doing is getting&nbsp;them down to a level that you can still maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:11.600
your professionalism. You can still work with the&nbsp;patient in a functional effective manner and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:16.640
can still take good emotional care of yourself.&nbsp;As you're going through your responsibilities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:22.560
commitments. You are going to have to deal with it, one way shape or form. Some way and what I'm saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:28.320
to you is, that the best way that you can possibly&nbsp;do it, is that step three, that you think in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:34.080
of preferences. I'd certainly like to not get&nbsp;AIDS. I would prefer that this not be a contagious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:42.480
disease. It would be better if I didn't have any&nbsp;even minuscule uncertainty about this. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:53.040
not the case. Consequently, I am concerned. I regret&nbsp;that, it's a serious issue and I am committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.160 --> 00:21:01.520
to doing a quality job. I want to be real clear&nbsp;with you about one thing. I am not suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:08.880
and I'm not suggesting that you believe, that&nbsp;if you did contract AIDS it wouldn't be awful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:19.840
This is one of the few experiences in the world,&nbsp;where in fact, the event really truly is awful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:26.000
There are very few things in the world that really&nbsp;truly are awful. I mean we think divorces are awful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:32.160
and we think this thing is awful. And that you know&nbsp;I lost my car in an accident. This is awful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:40.400
that's awful. You know, we we awfulize about things&nbsp;that frankly just pale by comparison. The whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:48.480
point about this, what we call rational thinking&nbsp;is not to eliminate your feelings. You can't. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:53.600
impossible to eliminate feelings. The simple goal&nbsp;is to get those emotional reactions down to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:58.480
level that you can't function professionally.&nbsp;That you can take good care of yourself

00:21:58.480 --> 00:21:59.668
emotionally.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:32.420
you

